<h2>The receivable is property; the remedy is status</h2>
<p>Indian law has long treated a debt as an actionable claim — property that Section 130 of the Transfer of Property Act, 1882 allows its holder to assign by a written instrument. A supplier&rsquo;s unpaid invoice can therefore be sold, pledged or discounted like any other asset, and an entire regulated industry exists to buy it: factors registered under the Factoring Regulation Act, 2011, and the Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) platforms on which MSME invoices are auctioned to financiers.</p>
<p>The MSMED Act, however, speaks a different language. Section 15 obliges the <em>buyer</em> to pay the <em>supplier</em> within the agreed period, capped at forty-five days from acceptance or deemed acceptance. Section 16 makes delayed payment carry compound interest, at monthly rests, at three times the bank rate. Section 17 entitles the supplier to recover the amount with that interest, and Section 18 lets a party to such a dispute refer it to the Micro and Small Enterprises Facilitation Council. Every link in that chain is tied to the statutory definition of &ldquo;supplier&rdquo; in Section 2(n) — a micro or small enterprise, with specific statutory extensions such as the National Small Industries Corporation and State Small Industries Corporations. The package of advantages is, in substance, a privilege of the enterprise&rsquo;s status, enacted to protect the smallest participants in the supply chain.</p>

<h2>What travels with an assignment — and what does not</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Passes to the assignee</h3>
    <p>The debt itself, as an actionable claim. Contractual interest under the supply terms. Interest already accrued as part of the money claim. The right to sue the buyer civilly, and the buyer&rsquo;s obligation to pay the assignee once notice of assignment is given.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Stays with the enterprise</h3>
    <p>The status of &ldquo;supplier&rdquo; under Section 2(n). The practical ability to invoke the Facilitation Council machinery in its own right, with its conciliation-then-arbitration sequence and the 75% pre-deposit that shields any award. The policy premium the Act reserves for micro and small enterprises.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The buyer&rsquo;s defences also survive the transfer: an assignee takes the claim subject to every equity available against the assignor — quality disputes, debit notes, set-offs. Assignment changes the creditor; it does not improve the claim.</p>

<h2>Three routes to liquidity, three risk profiles</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Route</th><th>How it works</th><th>Who pursues a defaulting buyer</th><th>MSMED machinery</th></tr>
  <tr><td>TReDS discounting</td><td>Invoice accepted by the buyer is auctioned to financiers; the enterprise is paid upfront</td><td>The financier recovers from the buyer; recourse depends on the facility terms</td><td>Not needed for the discounted invoice — payment is immediate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Factoring (Factoring Regulation Act, 2011)</td><td>Receivables are assigned to a registered factor, with or without recourse</td><td>The factor, as assignee, through contractual and civil remedies</td><td>Best not assumed to be available to the factor in its own name</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Retain and refer</td><td>The enterprise keeps the claim and files a Section 18 reference on the Samadhaan portal</td><td>The enterprise itself, with statutory interest compounding monthly</td><td>Fully available — council forum, Section 16 interest, Section 19 deposit shield</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Structuring the funding without losing the forum</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Assign proceeds, not the claim.</strong> A financing document can give the financier the fruits of the recovery while the enterprise remains the claimant before the Council — preserving the statutory character of the proceeding.</li>
  <li><strong>Use recourse structures for disputed dues.</strong> Clean, buyer-accepted invoices suit TReDS and non-recourse factoring; contested claims belong with the enterprise, funded on recourse terms.</li>
  <li><strong>Serve notice of assignment properly.</strong> Where the debt is assigned, the buyer must know whom to pay; payment made to the assignor without notice complicates everyone&rsquo;s position.</li>
  <li><strong>Sell the award, not the argument.</strong> Once a Facilitation Council award attains finality, it is enforceable as a decree and a transferee can execute it under Order XXI Rule 16 CPC — a far cleaner asset to transfer than a raw, contested receivable.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the group-restructuring trap.</strong> Business transfers, slump sales and successions should expressly deal with pending Samadhaan references; a claim filed by one entity and a supply made by another is an invitation to a maintainability objection.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The buyer&rsquo;s perspective</h2>
<p>For buyers, an assignment notice is a moment for care, not relief. The forty-five-day discipline of Section 15 applied while the enterprise held the debt, and interest that accrued in that period does not evaporate because the invoice later changed hands. Equally, a buyer facing a claim from an assignee is entitled to ask the threshold questions: is the assignment in writing and complete, has notice been given, and in what capacity is the claimant invoking which forum? The answers decide whether the proceeding belongs before a Facilitation Council, a commercial court, or nowhere at all in its present form.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Before any MSME receivable is monetised, map the dispute first. An undisputed, buyer-accepted invoice is a financeable asset; a disputed one is litigation in waiting, and the MSMED Act&rsquo;s forum and interest advantages are worth more than most discounts. Structure the funding around the enterprise&rsquo;s claim — not in place of it.</p>
</div>
